[White "4.6 Blockade"]
[Black "Example 298- Agrest- E. vs. Hillarp Persson- T.- Swedish Championship- Gothenburg 2005"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[FEN "8/8/8/1n4k1/4B3/1p1K1PP1/8/8 b - - 0 1"]

 { In this minor piece ending, Hillarp, a pawn down, found a clear-cut path to a draw, based on a blockade. } 1... { -KEY- } b2 $1 { The b-pawn is sacrificed in order to deflect White's king from the kingside. } 2. Kc2 Nd4+ 3. Kxb2 Ne2 $1 4. g4 Nf4 { Having provoked the g-pawn to a light square, Black can establish a dark-square blockade with his knight and king. } 5. Kc3 Nh3 6. Kd4 Kf4 ( 6... Nf4 { was just as good, since the knight will never run out of squares. By the way, such a blockade can fail if deployed at the edge of the board, as we already saw in Averbakh's study featured earlier in this chapter (see Example 249). } ) 
7. Kd5 Kg5 ( { Changing places with } 7... Ng5 { is also fine, of course. } ) 
8. Ke5 Nf4 9. Bf5 Nh3 10. Ke4 Nf4 { Note, too, that if Black's knight was a bishop and White's bishop was a knight, then the latter would win without much effort, relying on the knight's ability to touch squares of both colours. } 11. Ke3 Nh3 12. Bd3 Nf4 13. Bc4 Nh3 14. Bf1 { Seeing that the knight never runs out of squares, White agreed to a draw. } 
 1/2-1/2

