 { Thanks to his sounder pawn structure and superior minor piece, Black is better in the above position. } 1... { -KEY- } Ra6 $1 { The rook starts working on the weak white a- and c-pawns, while ceding the blocking square on d6 to its preferred user, the knight. } 2. a3 Ne8 $1 3. e4 { Thus White gets rid of his backward e-pawn and clears some space for the bishop. } fxe4 4. Bxe4 Nd6 5. Bd3 Ra4 6. Rc3 Kc7 7. Kf2 { Black's pieces were considerably improved over the last moves, but how to progress further? } b5 $1 { It might appear illogical to open the position for the enemy bishop, but this advance has several virtues: the d5-pawn is weakened, a passed pawn is created on the c-file, and Black's king will be able to cross the sixth rank. } 8. cxb5 Kb6 ( { Of course not } 8... Rxf4 $2 9. Ke3 { followed by Rxc5+, and Black loses his passed pawn. } ) 
9. Ke3 c4 $1 ( { Simplifying to a minor piece ending with an extra pawn. Another good option was } 9... Rd4 $5 { followed by ...Rxd5-h5. } ) 
10. Bc2 Nxb5 11. Bxa4 Nxc3 12. Be8 ( { Or } 12. Bc2 Kc5 13. d6 Kxd6 { when both } 14. f5 ( { and } 14. Kd4 Nb5 ) 
g5 $1 { favour Black - Hecht. } ) 
Kc5 $1 ( { King first! After } 12... Nxd5 $6 13. Kd4 c3 14. Ba4 { White would have more chances for survival, thanks to his active king. } ) 
13. d6 Nd5+ $1 ( { Less technical is } 13... Kxd6 $6 14. Kd4 { with similar play to the previous note. } ) 
14. Ke4 Nf6+ 15. Ke3 ( { Or } 15. Ke5 Nxe8 16. d7 Nd6 17. d8=N $11 c3 18. Ne6 Kc4 19. Nd4 Nb5 { winning - Hecht. } ) 
Kxd6 16. Bf7 Kc5 { With a healthy extra pawn and more active king, the rest is a matter of technique for Black. } 17. h4 Ng4+ 18. Kd2 ( { The desirable } 18. Ke4 { fails to } Nh6 19. Be6 Nf5 $1 20. h5 Ng3 { etc. } ) 
Kd4 19. h5 gxh5 20. Bxh5 Nf6 { In view of the imminent advance of the h-pawn, White resigned. } 
 0-1

