 { In this position Black's active knight on e4 hardly compensates for his defective pawn structure and bad bishop. Berg found a surprising solution, based on a blockade. } 1. { -KEY- } Bxe4 $5 { Aiming at a minor piece ending, where the problem with Black's bishop will be stronger felt. } ( { An obvious alternative was } 1. Bb3 { - Wedberg, but White perhaps wanted to avoid } Nc5 2. Bxd5 Bxd5 3. Rxd5 Na4 { with some slight counterplay for the pawn. } ) 
dxe4 2. Ne5 Rf6 { Covering the weak b6-pawn in advance. } ( { After } 2... Rfd8 3. Red1 Rxd4 4. Rxd4 { White has a big advantage. } ) 
3. Ng4 $1 { Heading for the great blockading square on e3. } ( { The obvious } 3. Rd7 { could be met by } Re8 $1 4. Ng4 Rf7 5. Rd6 h5 6. Ne3 Rf6 { etc. } ) 
Rg6 4. Red1 h5 5. Ne3 Rf8 6. Rd6 $1 { Limiting any enemy counterplay. } Rxd6 $6 ( 6... Rgf6 7. Rxf6 Rxf6 { was more stubborn, avoiding the minor piece ending. } ) 
7. Rxd6 Rf6 8. Rxf6 gxf6 9. Kh2 $1 { The king has found an excellent route to the action along the dark squares. } Kf7 ( { The restrictive } 9... h4 { fails to } 10. Nf5 ) 
10. Kg3 Kg6 11. Kf4 Bc6 12. Nf5 Bd7 13. Ne3 ( { There was nothing wrong with } 13. Nh4 Kf7 14. Kxe4 Bc6 15. Kf4 { but Berg has seen that by preparing a zugzwang he can win even more comfortably. } ) 
Bc6 14. h4 $1 a5 ( { Or } 14... Bb7 15. Nf5 { with a similar effect. } ) 
15. Nf5 Bd7 16. Nd4 $1 f5 17. Ke5 
 1-0

