 { It is no secret that the typical performer of the blockade is the knight, but occasionally other pieces can take up this task as well. Here are a few examples.With a healthy extra pawn and a sounder structure, White is much better, though the presence of opposite-coloured bishops gives Black some hope of survival. Bologan found a fine manoeuvre. } 1. { -KEY- } Bf5 $1 { Heading for e4, where the bishop will immobilize Black's e-pawn and control the whole board. Moreover, it can't be chased away from there. } g6 ( { a) Or } 1... Rd5 2. Rc2 $1 { with similar play. } ) 
 ( { b) But } 1... h6 { might have been a lesser evil, avoiding the exposure of the kingside pawns. } ) 
2. Rd7+ Kb6 3. Be4 Rb4 ( { The attempt to reduce material by } 3... Rxa2 4. Rxh7 Rxb3 { fails to } 5. Rc1 { - Bologan. } ) 
4. Re1 Rd4 ( { On this occasion, } 4... Rxa2 { runs into } 5. Rd6 $1 Bg5 6. Rxc6 Kb5 7. Re6 ) 
5. Rxh7 Rxa2 6. Rf7 Rd6 7. Bxg6 { With two extra pawns, the rest is a matter of technique for Bologan. } Rb2 8. Rb1 $1 Ra2 ( { After } 8... Rxb1 9. Bxb1 Rd1 10. Kg2 Rxb1 11. Rxf6 Rxb3 { the enemy pieces aren't well placed for a battle against the h-pawn. } ) 
9. Be4 Bd8 10. Rc1 Rb2 11. Rc3 Re2 12. Rc4 $1 ( { Bologan doesn't allow even the slightest counterplay after } 12. Bf3 Re1 13. Kg2 e4 { and instead reinforces his blockade. } ) 
Rb2 13. Rf3 Be7 14. Kg2 Rh6 15. h4 Rd2 16. Rfc3 Rdd6 17. Rc2 Rdf6 18. b4 Rd6 19. Kh3 Bf8 20. Kg4 $1 { With the clever plan of h4-h5 and Bg6, further restricting the enemy pieces. } Be7 21. h5 Bf8 22. Bg6 Bg7 23. Kf3 Rh8 24. g4 Rf8+ 25. Kg3 Rd4 26. Be4 { In this hopeless position, Black resigned. } 
 1-0

