 { In such IQP structures we often see a white knight occupying the blocking square on d4, as in Example 288 (Seirawan-Adianto) earlier in this chapter. Here, the knight already enjoys a good post on c3 where it pressurizes the d5-pawn, thus the d4-square becomes available for its fellow pieces. } 1. { -KEY- } Rd4 $1 { Expanding the rook's range while preventing any tactical shot with ...Nxf3 or ...d5-d4. } a6 ( { Unfortunately for Black, the desirable } 1... Nc6 $2 { now fails to } 2. Rxd5 ) 
2. Be2 b5 $6 { This advance only helps White to open a new front on the queenside. } ( 2... Ke7 { was preferable, with just a slight disadvantage after } 3. f4 { - Ftacnik. } ) 
3. a4 $1 bxa4 4. Rxa4 a5 5. Rd4 Nc4 $2 { Don't swap your active pieces! In the remainder of the game, the passive state of the b7-bishop will be strongly felt. } ( { The solid } 5... Bc6 { was better - Ftacnik. } ) 
6. Bxc4 $1 Rxc4 ( { Or } 6... dxc4 7. Rd7 Bc8 8. Ra7 { with a big advantage, thanks to the excellent rook on a7. } ) 
7. Rxc4 dxc4 8. e4 $1 { Preparing Ke3-d4. In Seirawan-Adianto we saw Black pay a high price for the delay of his king's arrival on the battlefield, and here the story is repeated. } Ke7 9. Ke3 Kd6 10. Kd4 Ba6 11. f4 $1 { Gaining some space and preparing to open a new front on the kingside. } g6 ( { If } 11... f6 { then } 12. Nd5 { heading for e3 - Ftacnik. } ) 
12. g5 hxg5 13. hxg5 Ke6 { Black finds himself in zugzwang. } ( 13... Kc6 { was no improvement because of } 14. Nd5 Kd6 15. Ne3 c3 $5 16. e5 $1 Kc6 17. Kxc3 Kc5 18. f5 Bb5 ( { on } 18... gxf5 { there follows the same reply; } ) 
19. e6 $1 Be8 20. e7 gxf5 21. Nxf5 Kc6 22. Kd4 a4 23. Ke5 { and White wins - Ftacnik. } ) 
14. Kc5 f6 15. gxf6 Kxf6 16. Nd5+ Kf7 17. Kb6 { White finally realizes his positional assets, and the rest is easy. } Bc8 18. Kxa5 Bg4 19. Kb4 Be2 20. Kc5 ( { Black resigned, a possible conclusion of the game being } 20. Kc5 Bf3 21. Kd4 g5 22. fxg5 Kg6 23. Ne3 Kxg5 24. Nxc4 { - Ftacnik. } ) 

 1-0

